        {
          "id": "derive-1-2",
          "claim": "The cold bath loses $Q_c$, the hot bath gains $Q_h$, and their entropy slopes are constant. Thus their entropy changes are $-Q_c/T_c$ and $Q_h/T_h$. The working system and work store have zero entropy change, so [F3] gives\n$\n-\\frac{Q_c}{T_c}+\\frac{Q_h}{T_h}=\\Delta S_{\\mathrm{tot}}\\ge0.\n$",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-2-1",
          "claim": "Since $T_c,T_h>0$, step 1.2 gives $Q_h\\ge Q_cT_h/T_c$. Substituting $Q_h=Q_c+W_{\\mathrm{in}}$ from step 1.1 yields\n$\nW_{\\mathrm{in}}\\ge Q_c\\frac{T_h-T_c}{T_c}.\n$",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-3-1",
          "claim": "Divide by $W_{\\mathrm{in}}>0$ and use $T_h-T_c>0$ to obtain $\\mathrm{COP}_R\\le T_c/(T_h-T_c)$. Equality is equivalent to $\\Delta S_{\\mathrm{tot}}=0$; a reversible cycle has this property by [F3].",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F3",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: if no admitted refrigerator cycle satisfies the hypotheses, the conditional conclusion has no instance."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement the strictly ordered positive temperatures make T_h-T_c positive, while W_in>0 supplies the coefficient-of-performance denominator. Zero entropy production is the allowed saturation case, not zero heat throughput.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Let an admitted refrigerator cycle in the sense of [[def-td-reservoir-cycle]] operate between fixed ideal-bath temperatures $0<T_c<T_h$, extract $Q_c>0$ from the cold bath, reject $Q_h>0$ to the hot bath, and consume $W_{\\mathrm{in}}>0$. Define its cooling coefficient of performance by $\\mathrm{COP}_R=Q_c/W_{\\mathrm{in}}$. Then\n$\n\\mathrm{COP}_R\\le\\frac{T_c}{T_h-T_c}.\n$\nEquality holds exactly when the total entropy change of the isolated compound is zero. A reversible cycle in the stated boundary model saturates the bound; no converse about reversibility is asserted.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "76fcdf4112cb02c6f3792e09fa38ab7ea707fc3ac8e925ab7d359328f045debf",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The definition and Statement require two distinct baths with Tc<Th; the one-bath case is excluded."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No degeneracy parameter enters the refrigerator bound."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Steps 1.1\u20131.2 use the working-system return endpoint and the full compound entropy endpoint balance."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "An admitted refrigerator cycle is assumed; the theorem does not assert that one exists."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 3.1 proves that zero total entropy change gives equality in the COP bound."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 3.1 derives the bound and equality equivalence from step 2.1 and the entropy balance in step 1.2."
        }
      ],
      "supplier_excerpts": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "supplier": "def-td-reservoir-cycle",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "excerpt": "Fix a working system of constitution $c$ and a closed process record $P\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{cl}}(c)$ with equilibrium endpoints as in [[def-td-process-and-protocol]]. A cycle of the working system is such a record with $x_P^-=x_P^+$; the reservoirs and work store need not return to their initial energy states.\n\nAn ideal thermal reservoir $b$ has an energy interval $J_b\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ measured in joules and a finite entropy function $S_b:J_b\\to\\mathbb R$ measured in $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$. On the energy interval admitted for a cycle, assume the exact affine law\n$\nS_b(E_b)=s_{b,0}+\\frac{E_b}{T_b}\n$\nfor a constant $s_{b,0}$ and fixed $T_b>0$ kelvins. Thus $S_b'(E_b)=1/T_b$ in the interior and every admitted endpoint change is exactly $\\Delta S_b=\\Delta E_b/T_b$. This constant is the absolute thermodynamic temperature under [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]]. The affine entropy law on the admitted interval is the ideal-bath model; a finite reservoir whose temperature changes is outside this definition.\n\nAn ideal work store is a named auxiliary with an energy coordinate $E_w$ measured in joules and an assigned entropy $S_w(E_w)=s_w$ constant on the energy interval used by the cycle. It exchanges only mechanical work and no matter or heat. Its energy may change, while its entropy change is zero by this model definition.\n\nA two-reservoir cycle contains one working-system cycle, one hot bath at $T_h$, one cold bath at $T_c$, and the ideal work store. They lie inside one isolated outer boundary in the sense of [[def-td-system-and-permitted-exchanges]], so the full compound has no external matter or energy transfer. There are no unlisted heat, work, matter, kinetic-energy, or external-potential-energy changes. The admitted process and transfer signs are those of [[def-td-process-and-protocol]]. The model declares the endpoint entropy split\n$\nS_{\\mathrm{tot}}=S_{\\mathrm{ws}}(x_{\\mathrm{ws}})+S_h(E_h)+S_{\\mathrm{cold}}(E_c)+S_w(E_w),\n$\nwhere $S_{\\mathrm{ws}}$ is the working-system entropy and the bath and work-store functions are those defined here. This additive constituent split is assumed to be one admitted by [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]] and is the entropy supplied to [[post-td-macroscopic-second-law]]; $S_w$ contributes zero change. Reversibility of the full record is as in [[def-td-reversible-process]].\n\nWhen the second law is applied to the cycle, the model also supplies an admitted closed process record for the full compound. At both endpoints its constituent preparations, with the declared contact controls locked or insulated, are an equilibrium macrostate for that record; its separately tracked center-of-mass kinetic and external-field potential energies have zero endpoint change. This total-compound record is the input to the second law; the working-system record $P$ is the input to its first-law balance.\n\nIn the heat-engine mode, the positive magnitudes $Q_h,Q_c$ in joules mean respectively heat taken from the hot bath into the working system and heat rejected by the working system to the cold bath; accordingly $\\Delta E_h=-Q_h$, $\\Delta E_c=Q_c$, $Q[P]=Q_h-Q_c$, and work output is $W_{\\mathrm{out}}=-W_{\\mathrm{on}}[P]$. In the refrigerator mode, $Q_c>0$ is heat taken from the cold bath into the working system, $Q_h>0$ is heat rejected to the hot bath, so $\\Delta E_c=-Q_c$, $\\Delta E_h=Q_h$, $Q[P]=Q_c-Q_h$, and work input is $W_{\\mathrm{in}}=W_{\\mathrm{on}}[P]$. These are idealized model conventions, not claims that a particular apparatus realizes the cycle.",
          "supplier_status": "draft",
          "supplier_proof_status": "not-applicable",
          "evidence_scope": "Exact current interface excerpt; records citation fit and status, not independent proof approval."
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "supplier": "post-td-first-law-energy",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "excerpt": "For every admitted $P\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{cl}}(s)$ with equilibrium endpoints $x_P^-,x_P^+\\in D_c$, fixed material constitution, zero change in center-of-mass kinetic energy, and zero change in separately tracked external-field potential energy in the fixed chart selected by the process model, the adopted model balance is\n$$U_c(x_P^+)-U_c(x_P^-)=Q[P]+W_{\\mathrm{on}}[P],$$\nwhere $U_c$ is the dimensioned energy-coordinate map of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]].\n\nHere the internal-energy coordinate is evaluated in the center-of-mass rest frame as in [[def-td-galilean-rest-frame]], while $Q[P]$ and $W_{\\mathrm{on}}[P]$ are the protocol transfers defined by [[def-td-process-and-protocol]], in joules as in [[def-td-si-quantities]]. The sign convention is positive $Q$ into the system and positive $W_{\\mathrm{on}}$ for work done on it. If $P$ is adiabatic, its defining condition is $Q[P]=0$, so the same adopted balance gives $W_{\\mathrm{on}}[P]=U_c(x_P^+)-U_c(x_P^-)$.\n\nThis equation is an adopted physical postulate of the stated framework. It is not deduced here from a mechanical model or from experimental observations. Records with matter exchange or nonzero changes in omitted kinetic or external-field energy require a separately specified balance and are outside this item.",
          "supplier_status": "draft",
          "supplier_proof_status": "not-applicable",
          "evidence_scope": "Exact current interface excerpt; records citation fit and status, not independent proof approval."
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "supplier": "post-td-macroscopic-second-law",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "excerpt": "For every admitted closed process record $P\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{cl}}(s)$ of an isolated compound, assume its declared outer boundary is isolated throughout, its material constitution is fixed, its center-of-mass kinetic and separately tracked external-field potential energies have zero endpoint change, and both endpoint preparations are equilibrium macrostates. Include every named auxiliary and work device in the compound, and restrict to constituent splits for which [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]] supplies the additive total entropy. The adopted macroscopic second law is\n\n$$S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x^+)-S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x^-)\\ge0,$$\n\nwhere $S_{\\mathrm{tot}}$ is the sum of the constituent entropies in [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]]. The process record and the zero-change conditions are those of [[def-td-process-and-protocol]]. The process may pass through nonequilibrium configurations; this postulate compares endpoint entropies only and assigns no entropy function to those intermediate configurations.\n\nFor an admitted reversible operation in the sense of [[def-td-reversible-process]], adopt zero total entropy change. An admitted irreversible adiabatic operation may have a strictly positive entropy increase, but strict increase is not required for every irreversible operation. The postulate does not state monotonicity for an open compound, an externally driven process, a subsystem that exchanges energy with an omitted environment, or every microscopic trajectory.",
          "supplier_status": "draft",
          "supplier_proof_status": "not-applicable",
          "evidence_scope": "Exact current interface excerpt; records citation fit and status, not independent proof approval."
        }
      ]
    },
