      "supplier_excerpts": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "supplier": "pthm-td-refrigerator-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "excerpt": "Let an admitted refrigerator cycle in the sense of [[def-td-reservoir-cycle]] operate between fixed ideal-bath temperatures $0<T_c<T_h$, extract $Q_c>0$ from the cold bath, reject $Q_h>0$ to the hot bath, and consume $W_{\\mathrm{in}}>0$. Define its cooling coefficient of performance by $\\mathrm{COP}_R=Q_c/W_{\\mathrm{in}}$. Then\n$\n\\mathrm{COP}_R\\le\\frac{T_c}{T_h-T_c}.\n$\nEquality holds exactly when the total entropy change of the isolated compound is zero. A reversible cycle in the stated boundary model saturates the bound; no converse about reversibility is asserted.",
          "supplier_status": "draft",
          "supplier_proof_status": "ai-generated",
          "evidence_scope": "Exact current interface excerpt; records citation fit and status, not independent proof approval."
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "supplier": "def-td-si-quantities",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "excerpt": "This framework uses the modern International System of Units (SI). A scalar quantity has a real numerical value with the stated unit; a vector quantity has a value in the stated real coordinate space with the stated component unit. The numerical coordinates used elsewhere on this page are divided by their displayed SI units before they are placed in a Euclidean coordinate space.\n\nThe model's energy coordinate $U$ and its process-transfer values $Q$ and $W_{\\mathrm{on}}$ are scalar energies measured in joules (J). Volume $V$ is a scalar measured in cubic metres (m\u00b3); amount of substance $n_j$ for species $j$ is a scalar measured in moles (mol); and a particle count $N_j$, when separately used in a microscopic model, is dimensionless. Time $t$ is measured in seconds (s), mass $M$ in kilograms (kg), and a translational velocity $\\mathbf v_{\\mathrm{cm}}$ is a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$ with components measured in metres per second (m/s). The physical meanings of $U,Q,W_{\\mathrm{on}}$ are fixed by the state and process definitions, not by their units alone.\n\nThe relevant exact SI relations are\n\n$$1\\,\\mathrm J=1\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m^2\\,s^{-2}},\\qquad 1\\,\\mathrm N=1\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-2}},\\qquad 1\\,\\mathrm{Pa}=1\\,\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}}.$$\n\nThe kelvin (K) is the SI unit of thermodynamic temperature. The Boltzmann constant has the exact defining value $k_{\\mathrm B}=1.380649\\times10^{-23}\\,\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$. The mole is the SI unit of amount of substance; the Avogadro constant has the exact defining value $N_{\\mathrm A}=6.02214076\\times10^{23}\\,\\mathrm{mol^{-1}}$. When a model uses particle counts, $n_j=N_j/N_{\\mathrm A}$. The molar gas constant is derived as $R=N_{\\mathrm A}k_{\\mathrm B}=8.31446261815324\\,\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}\\,K^{-1}}$. This framework does not set $k_{\\mathrm B}=1$. These SI definitions do not themselves introduce a thermodynamic temperature state function or entropy function.\n\nWithin the macroscopic framework, $(U,V,n_j)$ are adopted state coordinates. A quantity's status as primitive in this model is separate from whether its SI unit is a base or derived unit.",
          "supplier_status": "draft",
          "supplier_proof_status": "not-applicable",
          "evidence_scope": "Exact current interface excerpt; records citation fit and status, not independent proof approval."
        }
      ],
      "finite_checks": [
        {
          "calculation": "Th-Tc=60 K and Tc/(Th-Tc)=240/60=4; with Qc=1200 J, Win>=Qc/4=300 J.",
          "disposition": "Symbolic stipulated model calculation; not an appliance measurement."
        }
      ]
