    "cex-td-integrable-capacity-not-integer-power": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-td-response-functions",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a constitution $c$ and an open dimensionless subset $O\\subseteq D_c$ in the single-phase region of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. Its physical chart is $\\Omega=\\Phi_c(O)$, where $\\Phi_c$ is the SI coordinate map defined in [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]]. For fixed physical amounts $n=(n_1,\\ldots,n_r)$ with nonempty slice $\\Omega_n:=\\{(U,V):(U,V,n)\\in\\Omega\\}$, this slice is open in $\\mathbb R^2$. Use $T,p$ from [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]] as functions of the physical coordinates on $\\Omega_n$. For a response derivative, restrict to an open neighborhood in $\\Omega_n$ where the required natural-coordinate map is a $C^1$ diffeomorphism onto an open image, so its inverse expresses the physical $(U,V)$ as $C^1$ functions of those natural coordinates ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]). Specifically, $C_V$ uses $(U,V)\\mapsto(T,V)$ at fixed $n$, while $C_p$, $\\kappa_T$, and $\\alpha_p$ use $(U,V)\\mapsto(T,p)$ at fixed $n$. The existence of each such local chart is a hypothesis for its response derivative, not a consequence of this definition. Each derivative uses the indicated natural variables and holds all other listed coordinates fixed.\n\nThe **constant-volume heat capacity** and **constant-pressure heat capacity** of the whole system are, where the derivatives exist and are finite,\n\n$$C_V:=\\left(\\frac{\\partial U}{\\partial T}\\right)_{V,n},\\qquad H:=U+pV,\\qquad C_p:=\\left(\\frac{\\partial H}{\\partial T}\\right)_{p,n}.$$\n\nHere $H$ is the enthalpy in joules; both heat capacities are in $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$, not per mole. The **isothermal compressibility** and **isobaric thermal expansion coefficient** are\n\n$$\\kappa_T:=-\\frac{1}{V}\\left(\\frac{\\partial V}{\\partial p}\\right)_{T,n},\\qquad \\alpha_p:=\\frac{1}{V}\\left(\\frac{\\partial V}{\\partial T}\\right)_{p,n}.$$\n\nSince $V>0$ and the coordinates use SI units ([[def-td-si-quantities]]), $\\kappa_T$ has units $\\mathrm{Pa^{-1}}$ and $\\alpha_p$ has units $\\mathrm{K^{-1}}$. This definition asserts no sign for any response, no identity among them, and no finite value at a critical or otherwise singular chart.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a material constitution $c$, with $r$ named species and dimensionless equilibrium state domain $D_c\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{r+2}$ from [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. For $x=(\\hat U,\\hat V,\\hat n_1,\\ldots,\\hat n_r)\\in D_c$, define the diagonal SI coordinate map\n\n$$\\Phi_c(x):=(U_c(x),V_c(x),n_{c,1}(x),\\ldots,n_{c,r}(x))=(\\hat U\\,\\mathrm J,\\hat V\\,\\mathrm{m^3},\\hat n_1\\,\\mathrm{mol},\\ldots,\\hat n_r\\,\\mathrm{mol}).$$\n\nIn SI numerical coordinates this is a diagonal linear bijection with a diagonal linear inverse, so it maps ambient open sets to open sets and its inverse is defined on its image.\n\nChoose an open subset $O\\subseteq D_c$ in the ambient dimensionless Euclidean coordinates that is designated as a single-phase chart, and set $\\Omega:=\\Phi_c(O)$, an open chart of physical SI coordinate values. Here $U$ is the internal-energy coordinate evaluated in the center-of-mass rest frame of [[def-td-galilean-rest-frame]]. If $S_c:D_c\\to\\mathbb R$ is the entropy adopted by [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]], define its physical-coordinate representative on $\\Omega$ by\n\n$$S_c^{\\mathrm{SI}}:=S_c\\circ\\Phi_c^{-1}:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R.$$\n\nWhen its arguments are the physical coordinates $(U,V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r)$, write this representative as $S_c(U,V,n)$ for brevity. On $\\Omega$, assume the finite representative is $C^2$ and has $S_{c,U}>0$, as adopted by [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]]. Here $C^2$ means that all coordinate partial derivatives through order two exist and are continuous ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]). A coordinate partial such as $S_{c,U}$ is the ordinary derivative in the physical $U$ coordinate while every other physical coordinate is held fixed, as in [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]; in particular the conversion factor from $\\hat U$ is included.\n\nExplicitly, with the partial derivatives on the right taken in the dimensionless coordinates on $O$ while holding every other hatted coordinate fixed, the physical partials are\n\n$$S_{c,U}^{\\mathrm{SI}}=(1\\,\\mathrm J)^{-1}\\partial_{\\hat U}S_c,\\qquad S_{c,V}^{\\mathrm{SI}}=(1\\,\\mathrm{m^3})^{-1}\\partial_{\\hat V}S_c,\\qquad S_{c,n_j}^{\\mathrm{SI}}=(1\\,\\mathrm{mol})^{-1}\\partial_{\\hat n_j}S_c,$$\n\nusing the coordinate-conversion convention of [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]].\n\nDefine the physical-coordinate representatives of the **absolute thermodynamic temperature**, **entropic pressure**, and **molar chemical potential** of species $j$ by\n\n$$T_c^{\\mathrm{SI}}:=\\frac{1}{S_{c,U}^{\\mathrm{SI}}},\\qquad p_c^{\\mathrm{SI}}:=T_c^{\\mathrm{SI}}S_{c,V}^{\\mathrm{SI}},\\qquad \\mu_{c,j}^{\\mathrm{SI}}:=-T_c^{\\mathrm{SI}}S_{c,n_j}^{\\mathrm{SI}}.$$\n\nFor a state-domain point $x\\in O$, define the corresponding state functions by pullback, $T_c(x):=T_c^{\\mathrm{SI}}(\\Phi_c(x))$, $p_c(x):=p_c^{\\mathrm{SI}}(\\Phi_c(x))$, and $\\mu_{c,j}(x):=\\mu_{c,j}^{\\mathrm{SI}}(\\Phi_c(x))$. When the argument is a physical tuple $(U,V,n)$, write $T_c(U,V,n)$, $p_c(U,V,n)$, and $\\mu_{c,j}(U,V,n)$ for the physical-coordinate representatives. Thus both notations refer to the same state functions through $\\Phi_c$.\n\nThe entropy unit is $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$, the energy $U$ is in joules, $V$ is in cubic metres, and $n_j$ is in moles ([[def-td-si-quantities]]). Thus $S_{c,U}^{\\mathrm{SI}}$ has units $\\mathrm{K^{-1}}$, $T_c$ is finite and positive in kelvins, $S_{c,V}^{\\mathrm{SI}}$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}\\,m^{-3}}$ and $p_c$ has units pascals, and $S_{c,n_j}^{\\mathrm{SI}}$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}\\,mol^{-1}}$ and $\\mu_{c,j}$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}}$. If a microscopic model instead uses a dimensionless particle count $N_j=N_{\\!A}n_j$, the corresponding per-particle chemical potential is $\\mu_{c,j}/N_{\\!A}$ joules, with the exact SI Avogadro constant from [[def-td-si-quantities]].\n\nThese are scalar state functions of the stated rest-frame equilibrium chart. The pressure in this definition is a derivative-defined conjugate; identifying it with force per area requires a separate adopted mechanical coupling. Neither an empirical thermometer calibration nor any equation of state is supplied by this definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-td-si-quantities",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "This framework uses the modern International System of Units (SI). A scalar quantity has a real numerical value with the stated unit; a vector quantity has a value in the stated real coordinate space with the stated component unit. The numerical coordinates used elsewhere on this page are divided by their displayed SI units before they are placed in a Euclidean coordinate space.\n\nThe model's energy coordinate $U$ and its process-transfer values $Q$ and $W_{\\mathrm{on}}$ are scalar energies measured in joules (J). Volume $V$ is a scalar measured in cubic metres (m\u00b3); amount of substance $n_j$ for species $j$ is a scalar measured in moles (mol); and a particle count $N_j$, when separately used in a microscopic model, is dimensionless. Time $t$ is measured in seconds (s), mass $M$ in kilograms (kg), and a translational velocity $\\mathbf v_{\\mathrm{cm}}$ is a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$ with components measured in metres per second (m/s). The physical meanings of $U,Q,W_{\\mathrm{on}}$ are fixed by the state and process definitions, not by their units alone.\n\nThe relevant exact SI relations are\n\n$$1\\,\\mathrm J=1\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m^2\\,s^{-2}},\\qquad 1\\,\\mathrm N=1\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-2}},\\qquad 1\\,\\mathrm{Pa}=1\\,\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}}.$$\n\nThe kelvin (K) is the SI unit of thermodynamic temperature. The Boltzmann constant has the exact defining value $k_{\\mathrm B}=1.380649\\times10^{-23}\\,\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$. The mole is the SI unit of amount of substance; the Avogadro constant has the exact defining value $N_{\\mathrm A}=6.02214076\\times10^{23}\\,\\mathrm{mol^{-1}}$. When a model uses particle counts, $n_j=N_j/N_{\\mathrm A}$. The molar gas constant is derived as $R=N_{\\mathrm A}k_{\\mathrm B}=8.31446261815324\\,\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}\\,K^{-1}}$. This framework does not set $k_{\\mathrm B}=1$. These SI definitions do not themselves introduce a thermodynamic temperature state function or entropy function.\n\nWithin the macroscopic framework, $(U,V,n_j)$ are adopted state coordinates. A quantity's status as primitive in this model is separate from whether its SI unit is a base or derived unit.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "For each admitted material constitution $c$, the model adopts a finite scalar function\n\n$$S_c:D_c\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R\\ \\text{with values measured in }\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}},$$\n\non the equilibrium state domain $D_c$ of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. This framework treats $S_c$ as primitive constitutive data: its existence and form are assumed, not derived from the word entropy, statistical mechanics, or experimental evidence.\n\nOn every specified convex region $C\\subseteq D_c$ in the dimensionless coordinates of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]], $S_c$ is continuous and concave, meaning that for all $x,y\\in C$ and every $\\alpha\\in[0,1]$ ([[def-interval]]),\n\n$$S_c((1-\\alpha)x+\\alpha y)\\ge (1-\\alpha)S_c(x)+\\alpha S_c(y).$$\n\nThe domain assumptions are local to the material and region named; no unlisted coordinate tuple is presumed physically realizable. The input of $S_c$ is the represented state $\\pi_c(e)$; when the function is written using physical coordinates $(U,V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r)$, those coordinates mean the SI values whose dimensionless numerical tuple is $\\pi_c(e)$. For independent separated macroscopic systems $A,B$ admitted by [[post-td-bulk-additivity]], entropy is additive under that postulate's composition map $\\kappa_{AB}$:\n\n$$S_{A\\sqcup B}(\\kappa_{AB}(x_A,x_B))=S_A(x_A)+S_B(x_B).$$\n\nOn each separately designated open, single-phase chart in the physical coordinates $(U,V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r)$, assume the numerical representative of $S_c$ is $C^2$ in the sense of [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]: its first and second coordinate partial derivatives exist and are continuous. The partial derivative $S_U$ at fixed $V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r$ is the ordinary partial derivative in the $U$ coordinate, as in [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]], and is assumed finite and strictly positive. The numerical chart input uses $\\hat U=U/(1\\,\\mathrm J)$; consequently $S_U=(1\\,\\mathrm J)^{-1}\\partial S_c/\\partial\\hat U$ and has units $\\mathrm{K^{-1}}$. The other physical coordinates are similarly represented using the fixed SI units of [[def-td-si-quantities]]. No differentiability, strict concavity, or positive $S_U$ is asserted outside those charts. This item does not itself define a thermometer calibration or claim a temperature law.\n\nFor the tagged bulk family with scaling maps $\\delta_\\lambda$ and domains $B_{c,\\lambda}$ from [[post-td-bulk-additivity]], also adopt, for every admitted $\\lambda>0$ and $x\\in B_{c,\\lambda}$,\n\n$$S_{c^{(\\lambda)}}(\\delta_\\lambda x)=\\lambda S_c(x).$$\n\nThis is a bulk homogeneity assumption for tagged scaled systems, not exact finite-size scaling for every body. Lieb\u2013Yngvason develop a different accessibility-based route in which entropy is represented from additional operational hypotheses; this framework does not claim that their derivation proves the present primitive entropy postulate.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "def-real-power",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $a>0$ and $x\\in\\mathbb R$, define\n$$a^x:=\\exp(x\\log a).$$\nFor the supplementary zero-base convention, define $0^x:=0$ when $x>0$.\n\nThe expressions $0^0$ and $0^x$ for $x<0$ are left undefined. Negative bases are not assigned arbitrary real powers.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $a>0$, the function $x\\mapsto a^x$ is continuous on $\\mathbb R$ and\n$$(a^x)'=a^x\\log a.$$\nFor $\\alpha\\in\\mathbb R$, the function $x\\mapsto x^\\alpha$ is continuous and differentiable on $(0,\\infty)$, with\n$$(x^\\alpha)'=\\alpha x^{\\alpha-1}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L2",
          "source": "thm-chain-sum-product-and-composition-rules-for-banach-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $U \\subseteq X$ be open in a real Banach space $X$, and let $Y$, $Z$, $W$ be\nreal Banach spaces. Then:\n\n1. **Sum rule.** If $f_1, f_2 : U \\to Y$ are Fr\u00e9chet differentiable at $x \\in U$\n   and $a, b \\in \\mathbb R$, then $af_1+bf_2$ is Fr\u00e9chet differentiable at $x$\n   with $D(af_1+bf_2)(x) = a\\,Df_1(x) + b\\,Df_2(x)$.\n2. **Bounded-bilinear product rule.** If $f : U \\to Y$ and $g : U \\to Z$ are\n   Fr\u00e9chet differentiable at $x$, and $B : Y \\times Z \\to W$ is bounded\n   bilinear, then $U \\ni u \\mapsto B(f(u),g(u))$ is Fr\u00e9chet differentiable at\n   $x$ and\n\n$$D\\bigl(B(f,g)\\bigr)(x)h = B\\bigl(Df(x)h,\\ g(x)\\bigr) + B\\bigl(f(x),\\ Dg(x)h\\bigr) \\qquad (h \\in X).$$\n\n3. **Chain rule.** If $f : U \\to Y$ is Fr\u00e9chet differentiable at $x$, if\n   $W_0 \\subseteq Y$ is an open set with $f[U] \\subseteq W_0$, and if\n   $g : W_0 \\to Z$ is Fr\u00e9chet differentiable at $f(x)$, then\n   $g \\circ f$ is Fr\u00e9chet differentiable at $x$ and\n   $$D(g \\circ f)(x) = Dg\\bigl(f(x)\\bigr)\\,Df(x).$$\n\nNo continuity of any derivative map is assumed; these are pointwise statements\nabout one $x$ at a time.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L3",
          "source": "def-natural-logarithm",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, define $\\log x$ to be the unique real $y$ such that $\\exp(y)=x$. This is well-defined because [[cor-exponential-is-a-bijection-onto-positive-reals]] states that $\\exp:\\mathbb R\\to(0,\\infty)$ is a bijection.\n\nThus $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is the inverse function of $\\exp$. In particular, $\\log(\\exp y)=y$ for every $y\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\exp(\\log x)=x$ for every $x>0$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L3",
          "source": "thm-natural-logarithm-laws",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The function $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous and strictly increasing, is onto $\\mathbb R$, and satisfies, for $x,y>0$,\n$$\\log(xy)=\\log x+\\log y,\\qquad \\log(x/y)=\\log x-\\log y,\\qquad \\log(1/x)=-\\log x.$$\nAlso $\\log 1=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L3",
          "source": "thm-exponential-is-strictly-increasing",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The exponential function is continuous and strictly increasing on $\\mathbb R$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L4",
          "source": "def-ordered-field",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "An **ordered field** is a field $F$ ([[def-field]]) together with a subset\n$P \\subseteq F$, the set of **positive** elements (the *positive cone*),\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(O1) Trichotomy.** For each $x \\in F$, exactly one of the following holds:\n  $x \\in P$, or $x = 0$, or $-x \\in P$.\n- **(O2) Closure.** If $x, y \\in P$ then $x + y \\in P$ and $x \\cdot y \\in P$.\n\nWe write $x > 0$ for $x \\in P$ (read \"$x$ is positive\"), and define the order by\n\n$$a < b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; b - a \\in P, \\qquad a \\le b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; a < b \\text{ or } a = b.$$\n\nThus $a > b$ means $b < a$, and $a \\ge b$ means $b \\le a$. An element $x$ with\n$-x \\in P$ (equivalently $x < 0$) is called **negative**.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L4",
          "source": "thm-reals-ordered-field",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The relation of [[def-real-order]] is well defined and makes $\\mathbb{R}$\n([[thm-reals-field]]) a totally ordered field.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L4",
          "source": "lem-of-add-order",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) with positive cone $P$, and\nlet $a, b, c, d \\in F$.\n\n1. **Translation invariance.** If $a < b$ then $a + c < b + c$.\n2. **Adding inequalities.** If $a < b$ and $c < d$ then $a + c < b + d$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L5",
          "source": "def-convex-concave-and-midpoint-convex-functions",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an interval ([[def-interval]]) and let $f:I\\to\\mathbb R$. For $x,y\\in I$ and $\\lambda\\in[0,1]$, the point $\\lambda x+(1-\\lambda)y$ belongs to $I$. The function $f$ is **convex** when the convex-combination inequality holds for every weight in $[0,1]$:\n\n$$f(\\lambda x+(1-\\lambda)y)\\le \\lambda f(x)+(1-\\lambda)f(y).$$\n\nIt is **strictly convex** when this inequality is strict whenever $x\\ne y$ and $0<\\lambda<1$. It is **concave** (respectively **strictly concave**) when $-f$ is convex (respectively strictly convex). It is **midpoint convex** when the displayed inequality is required only at $\\lambda=1/2$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L6",
          "source": "cor-second-derivative-characterises-convexity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $f:I\\to\\mathbb R$ is twice differentiable on an open interval, then $f$ is convex if and only if $f''(x)\\ge0$ for every $x\\in I$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L7",
          "source": "thm-continuous-implies-integrable",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a < b$ be reals and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be continuous on $[a,b]$\n([[def-continuity-real]]). Then $f$ is bounded ([[def-bounded-set]]) and Riemann\nintegrable on $[a,b]$ ([[def-darboux-integral]]).\n\n**The proof gives more than integrability: it gives a partition that works.** For\nevery real $\\varepsilon > 0$ the uniform partition into $N$ parts already\nsatisfies $U(f,P) - L(f,P) < \\varepsilon$, as soon as $N$ is large enough that\n$(b-a)/\\iota(N)$ is below the $\\delta$ that uniform continuity supplies for\n$\\varepsilon/\\bigl(2(b-a)\\bigr)$. Uniform continuity is exactly what makes one\n$\\delta$ serve all $N$ subintervals at once, and it is the only place where the\ncompactness of $[a,b]$ is used.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L8",
          "source": "thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a<b$. Suppose $G:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b)$. If $f:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb R$ is Riemann integrable and\n\n$$f(x)=G'(x)\\qquad(a<x<b),$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\\int_a^b f=G(b)-G(a).$$\n\nNo derivative of $G$ at either endpoint is assumed, and the two endpoint values assigned to the integrable extension $f$ do not enter the conclusion.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "proof-step-1-1",
          "claim": "Repeated use of the power derivative [L1] and scalar chain, sum, and product rules [L2] shows that $S_c$ is $C^2$ on the positive open chart. Differentiating $\\theta(U)$ gives $\\theta_U=(C_*T_*)^{-1}\\theta^{-1/2}$, so $S_U=C_*\\theta^{-1/2}\\theta_U=1/(T_*\\theta)$ and $S_{UU}=-(C_*T_*^2)^{-1}\\theta^{-5/2}<0$. At fixed $V,n$, [L6] applied to $-S_c$ shows that $S_c$ is concave as a function of $U$; because $S_c$ is independent of $V,n$, the same convex-combination inequality holds on the full product chart. Thus the entropy is concave there and $S_U$ is finite and positive, as required by [F4]. By [F2], the absolute temperature is $T=1/S_U=T_*\\theta$. Differentiating the explicit branch functions $U(T),S(T)$ in [A1] by [L1,L2] gives\n$$U_T=C_*\\theta^{1/2},\\qquad S_T=\\frac{C_*}{T_*}\\theta^{-1/2}.$$\nThe response definition [F1] now gives $C_V=U_T=C_*\\theta^{1/2}=T S_T$. The displayed inverse branch and these continuous derivatives give a valid local response chart.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "L1",
            "L2",
            "L6",
            "F4",
            "F2",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "L5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-2-1",
          "claim": "For $0<\\epsilon<T$, the integrand $C_V(t)/t$ is continuous on $[\\epsilon,T]$ and is Riemann integrable there by [L7]; it agrees on its interior with the derivative of $2C_*\\sqrt{t/T_*}$. By [L8],\n$$\\int_\\epsilon^T\\frac{C_V(t)}{t}\\,dt=2C_*\\left(\\sqrt{T/T_*}-\\sqrt{\\epsilon/T_*}\\right).$$\nAs $\\epsilon\\downarrow0$, this tends to $2C_*\\sqrt{\\theta}=S(T)-S_0$, so the improper entropy integral converges and the entropy has the finite limit $S_0$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "L7",
            "L8",
            "A1",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-3-1",
          "claim": "Fix any dimensionless real exponent $q\\ge1$, any proposed $M>0$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$, and any dimensionless $\\delta\\in(0,1]$. Since $0<\\theta<1$ implies $\\log\\theta<0$ by [L3], ordered-field arithmetic [L4] gives $(q-1)\\log\\theta\\le0$ and hence $q\\log\\theta\\le\\log\\theta$. Thus $\\theta^q=\\exp(q\\log\\theta)\\le\\exp(\\log\\theta)=\\theta$ by [L1,L3]. Choose $0<\\theta<\\min\\{\\delta,(C_*/M)^2,1\\}$. Then $M\\theta^q\\le M\\theta<C_*\\sqrt{\\theta}=C_V(T)$, so the proposed bound fails at this temperature. As $M$ and $\\delta$ were arbitrary, no constants in the stated big-$O$ definition work for this $q$; since $q\\ge1$ was arbitrary, the assertion fails for every real exponent $q\\ge1$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "L3",
            "L4",
            "L1",
            "A1",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: the constructed positive-temperature branch on (0,T*) is nonempty because T*>0."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Counterexample uses positive T and theta=T/T_*; zero is a one-sided limiting endpoint, not a state with a defined 1/T factor. Its improper entropy integral is evaluated through positive cutoffs.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement refuted",
            "source_excerpt": "For a positive-temperature fixed-$V,n$ chart, write $\\theta=T/T_*$. For a dimensionless real exponent $q\\ge1$, the notation $C_V(T)=O(\\theta^q)$ as $\\theta\\downarrow0$ means that there exist a constant $M>0$ measured in $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$ and a dimensionless $\\delta\\in(0,1]$ such that $|C_V(T)|\\le M\\theta^q$ whenever $0<\\theta<\\delta$. Finite convergence of the entropy integral alone does not imply such a bound for any real $q\\ge1$, even when the entropy has a finite limit and the local response-chart identities apply. The displayed branch is a formal chart calculation, not a claim that a particular substance realizes the chart.\n\nThe displayed entropy is $S^{\\rm SI}$ in physical SI coordinates. For this constructed local model, choose the positive-amount open neighborhood stated here (or any positive neighborhood of the fixed amount, extending this entropy independently of amount) and let $O_c$ be the numerical preimage of that product patch under $\\Phi_c(\\hat U,\\hat V,\\hat n)=(\\hat U\\,\\mathrm J,\\hat V\\,\\mathrm{m^3},\\hat n\\,\\mathrm{mol})$. Declare $O_c$ as the model's local state-domain patch and set $S_c=S^{\\rm SI}\\circ\\Phi_c$. The derivatives below are those of $S^{\\rm SI}=S_c\\circ\\Phi_c^{-1}$ on $\\Phi_c(O_c)$, exactly as in [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]]. This supplies the numerical-to-SI chart identification for the formal witness, with no assertion of a measured material or a global constitutive extension.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "f75cfdbb6c508e132b4b856ec840c2b0917d11814b9abffaaaa1235b2ddf4671",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Counterexample proof3.1 includes the least real exponent q=1 and refutes the bound for every real q>=1, not just integer exponents. The positive numerical-to-SI response patch and finite limiting entropy are unchanged.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement refuted",
            "source_excerpt": "For a positive-temperature fixed-$V,n$ chart, write $\\theta=T/T_*$. For a dimensionless real exponent $q\\ge1$, the notation $C_V(T)=O(\\theta^q)$ as $\\theta\\downarrow0$ means that there exist a constant $M>0$ measured in $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$ and a dimensionless $\\delta\\in(0,1]$ such that $|C_V(T)|\\le M\\theta^q$ whenever $0<\\theta<\\delta$. Finite convergence of the entropy integral alone does not imply such a bound for any real $q\\ge1$, even when the entropy has a finite limit and the local response-chart identities apply. The displayed branch is a formal chart calculation, not a claim that a particular substance realizes the chart.\n\nThe displayed entropy is $S^{\\rm SI}$ in physical SI coordinates. For this constructed local model, choose the positive-amount open neighborhood stated here (or any positive neighborhood of the fixed amount, extending this entropy independently of amount) and let $O_c$ be the numerical preimage of that product patch under $\\Phi_c(\\hat U,\\hat V,\\hat n)=(\\hat U\\,\\mathrm J,\\hat V\\,\\mathrm{m^3},\\hat n\\,\\mathrm{mol})$. Declare $O_c$ as the model's local state-domain patch and set $S_c=S^{\\rm SI}\\circ\\Phi_c$. The derivatives below are those of $S^{\\rm SI}=S_c\\circ\\Phi_c^{-1}$ on $\\Phi_c(O_c)$, exactly as in [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]]. This supplies the numerical-to-SI chart identification for the formal witness, with no assertion of a measured material or a global constitutive extension.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "f75cfdbb6c508e132b4b856ec840c2b0917d11814b9abffaaaa1235b2ddf4671",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
        {
