      ],
      "supplier_excerpts": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "supplier": "def-td-response-functions",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "excerpt": "Fix a constitution $c$ and a smooth physical-coordinate chart in $D_c$ from [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]], with fixed amounts $n=(n_1,\\ldots,n_r)$ and $T,p$ as in [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]]. A **valid response chart** is an open neighborhood on which the relevant independent coordinates used below have a $C^1$ inverse to the physical $(U,V)$ coordinates; a $C^1$ diffeomorphism has a $C^1$ inverse on open neighborhoods by [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]. The existence of such a chart is a hypothesis for a response derivative, not a consequence of this definition. The derivatives use the indicated natural variables and hold all other listed coordinates fixed.\n\nThe **constant-volume heat capacity** and **constant-pressure heat capacity** of the whole system are, where the derivatives exist and are finite,\n\n$$C_V:=\\left(\\frac{\\partial U}{\\partial T}\\right)_{V,n},\\qquad H:=U+pV,\\qquad C_p:=\\left(\\frac{\\partial H}{\\partial T}\\right)_{p,n}.$$\n\nHere $H$ is the enthalpy in joules; both heat capacities are in $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$, not per mole. The **isothermal compressibility** and **isobaric thermal expansion coefficient** are\n\n$$\\kappa_T:=-\\frac{1}{V}\\left(\\frac{\\partial V}{\\partial p}\\right)_{T,n},\\qquad \\alpha_p:=\\frac{1}{V}\\left(\\frac{\\partial V}{\\partial T}\\right)_{p,n}.$$\n\nSince $V>0$ and the coordinates use SI units ([[def-td-si-quantities]]), $\\kappa_T$ has units $\\mathrm{Pa^{-1}}$ and $\\alpha_p$ has units $\\mathrm{K^{-1}}$. This definition asserts no sign for any response, no identity among them, and no finite value at a critical or otherwise singular chart.",
          "supplier_status": "draft",
          "supplier_proof_status": "not-applicable",
          "evidence_scope": "Exact current interface excerpt; records citation fit and status, not independent proof approval."
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "supplier": "def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "excerpt": "Fix a material constitution $c$ and its equilibrium state domain $D_c$ from [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. Let $\\Omega\\subseteq D_c$ be a specified open, single-phase chart in physical SI coordinates $(U,V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r)$, with $U$ evaluated in the center-of-mass rest frame of [[def-td-galilean-rest-frame]]. On $\\Omega$, use the finite $C^2$ entropy function $S_c$ and the strict positivity $S_{c,U}>0$ adopted by [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]]. Here $C^2$ means that all coordinate partial derivatives through order two exist and are continuous ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]). A coordinate partial such as $S_{c,U}$ is the ordinary derivative in that coordinate direction while every other physical coordinate is held fixed, as in [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]].\n\nDefine the **absolute thermodynamic temperature**, **entropic pressure**, and **molar chemical potential** of species $j$ by\n\n$$T_c:=\\frac{1}{S_{c,U}},\\qquad p_c:=T_cS_{c,V},\\qquad \\mu_{c,j}:=-T_cS_{c,n_j}.$$\n\nThe entropy unit is $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$, the energy $U$ is in joules, $V$ is in cubic metres, and $n_j$ is in moles ([[def-td-si-quantities]]). Thus $S_{c,U}$ has units $\\mathrm{K^{-1}}$, $T_c$ is finite and positive in kelvins, $S_{c,V}$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}\\,m^{-3}}$ and $p_c$ has units pascals, and $S_{c,n_j}$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}\\,mol^{-1}}$ and $\\mu_{c,j}$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}}$. If a microscopic model instead uses a dimensionless particle count $N_j=N_{\\!A}n_j$, the corresponding per-particle chemical potential is $\\mu_{c,j}/N_{\\!A}$ joules, with the exact SI Avogadro constant from [[def-td-si-quantities]].\n\nThese are scalar state functions of the stated rest-frame equilibrium chart. The pressure in this definition is a derivative-defined conjugate; identifying it with force per area requires a separate adopted mechanical coupling. Neither an empirical thermometer calibration nor any equation of state is supplied by this definition.",
          "supplier_status": "draft",
          "supplier_proof_status": "not-applicable",
          "evidence_scope": "Exact current interface excerpt; records citation fit and status, not independent proof approval."
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "supplier": "def-td-si-quantities",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "excerpt": "This framework uses the modern International System of Units (SI). A scalar quantity has a real numerical value with the stated unit; a vector quantity has a value in the stated real coordinate space with the stated component unit. The numerical coordinates used elsewhere on this page are divided by their displayed SI units before they are placed in a Euclidean coordinate space.\n\nThe model's energy coordinate $U$ and its process-transfer values $Q$ and $W_{\\mathrm{on}}$ are scalar energies measured in joules (J). Volume $V$ is a scalar measured in cubic metres (m\u00b3); amount of substance $n_j$ for species $j$ is a scalar measured in moles (mol); and a particle count $N_j$, when separately used in a microscopic model, is dimensionless. Time $t$ is measured in seconds (s), mass $M$ in kilograms (kg), and a translational velocity $\\mathbf v_{\\mathrm{cm}}$ is a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$ with components measured in metres per second (m/s). The physical meanings of $U,Q,W_{\\mathrm{on}}$ are fixed by the state and process definitions, not by their units alone.\n\nThe relevant exact SI relations are\n\n$$1\\,\\mathrm J=1\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m^2\\,s^{-2}},\\qquad 1\\,\\mathrm N=1\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-2}},\\qquad 1\\,\\mathrm{Pa}=1\\,\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}}.$$\n\nThe kelvin (K) is the SI unit of thermodynamic temperature. The Boltzmann constant has the exact defining value $k_{\\mathrm B}=1.380649\\times10^{-23}\\,\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$. The mole is the SI unit of amount of substance; the Avogadro constant has the exact defining value $N_{\\mathrm A}=6.02214076\\times10^{23}\\,\\mathrm{mol^{-1}}$. When a model uses particle counts, $n_j=N_j/N_{\\mathrm A}$. The molar gas constant is derived as $R=N_{\\mathrm A}k_{\\mathrm B}=8.31446261815324\\,\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}\\,K^{-1}}$. This framework does not set $k_{\\mathrm B}=1$. These SI definitions do not themselves introduce a thermodynamic temperature state function or entropy function.\n\nWithin the macroscopic framework, $(U,V,n_j)$ are adopted state coordinates. A quantity's status as primitive in this model is separate from whether its SI unit is a base or derived unit.",
          "supplier_status": "draft",
          "supplier_proof_status": "not-applicable",
          "evidence_scope": "Exact current interface excerpt; records citation fit and status, not independent proof approval."
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "supplier": "post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "excerpt": "For each admitted material constitution $c$, the model adopts a finite scalar function\n\n$$S_c:D_c\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R\\ \\text{with values measured in }\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}},$$\n\non the equilibrium state domain $D_c$ of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. This framework treats $S_c$ as primitive constitutive data: its existence and form are assumed, not derived from the word entropy, statistical mechanics, or experimental evidence.\n\nOn every specified convex region $C\\subseteq D_c$ in the dimensionless coordinates of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]], $S_c$ is continuous and concave, meaning that for all $x,y\\in C$ and every $\\alpha\\in[0,1]$ ([[def-interval]]),\n\n$$S_c((1-\\alpha)x+\\alpha y)\\ge (1-\\alpha)S_c(x)+\\alpha S_c(y).$$\n\nThe domain assumptions are local to the material and region named; no unlisted coordinate tuple is presumed physically realizable. The input of $S_c$ is the represented state $\\pi_c(e)$; when the function is written using physical coordinates $(U,V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r)$, those coordinates mean the SI values whose dimensionless numerical tuple is $\\pi_c(e)$. For independent separated macroscopic systems $A,B$ admitted by [[post-td-bulk-additivity]], entropy is additive under that postulate's composition map $\\kappa_{AB}$:\n\n$$S_{A\\sqcup B}(\\kappa_{AB}(x_A,x_B))=S_A(x_A)+S_B(x_B).$$\n\nOn each separately designated open, single-phase chart in the physical coordinates $(U,V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r)$, assume the numerical representative of $S_c$ is $C^2$ in the sense of [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]: its first and second coordinate partial derivatives exist and are continuous. The partial derivative $S_U$ at fixed $V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r$ is the ordinary partial derivative in the $U$ coordinate, as in [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]], and is assumed finite and strictly positive. The numerical chart input uses $\\hat U=U/(1\\,\\mathrm J)$; consequently $S_U=(1\\,\\mathrm J)^{-1}\\partial S_c/\\partial\\hat U$ and has units $\\mathrm{K^{-1}}$. The other physical coordinates are similarly represented using the fixed SI units of [[def-td-si-quantities]]. No differentiability, strict concavity, or positive $S_U$ is asserted outside those charts. This item does not itself define a thermometer calibration or claim a temperature law.\n\nFor the tagged bulk family with scaling maps $\\delta_\\lambda$ and domains $B_{c,\\lambda}$ from [[post-td-bulk-additivity]], also adopt, for every admitted $\\lambda>0$ and $x\\in B_{c,\\lambda}$,\n\n$$S_{c^{(\\lambda)}}(\\delta_\\lambda x)=\\lambda S_c(x).$$\n\nThis is a bulk homogeneity assumption for tagged scaled systems, not exact finite-size scaling for every body. Lieb\u2013Yngvason develop a different accessibility-based route in which entropy is represented from additional operational hypotheses; this framework does not claim that their derivation proves the present primitive entropy postulate.",
          "supplier_status": "draft",
          "supplier_proof_status": "not-applicable",
          "evidence_scope": "Exact current interface excerpt; records citation fit and status, not independent proof approval."
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "supplier": "def-td-legendre-potentials",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "excerpt": "Fix a material constitution $c$ with finite species list $(1,\\ldots,r)$ and a local energy representation $U_c=U_c(s,V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r)$ from [[pthm-td-local-energy-representation]]. Here $s$ is entropy in $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$, $V>0$ is volume in $\\mathrm{m^3}$, each $n_j>0$ is in $\\mathrm{mol}$, $U_c$ is in joules, and the conjugates are $T=U_{c,s}$ in kelvins, $p=-U_{c,V}$ in pascals, and $\\mu_j=U_{c,n_j}$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}}$. The derivatives hold every other listed natural coordinate fixed; the physical-coordinate and rest-frame conventions are those of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]] and [[def-td-si-quantities]].\n\nFor an open chart, a **local Legendre transformation** means the following partial transformation of $U_c$: select one or more of its extensive coordinates $q$, take their conjugate coordinates $x=D_qU_c$, and use only a neighborhood on which $(q,z)\\mapsto(x,z)$, with $z$ the untransformed coordinates, has a $C^1$ inverse. Such a neighborhood is a valid local transform chart in the sense of [[def-td-response-functions]]. No inverse or potential as a function of the proposed natural coordinates is asserted outside that chart.\n\nOn their respective valid charts define the enthalpy, Helmholtz potential, Gibbs potential, and grand potential by\n\n$$H_c:=U_c+pV,\\qquad F_c:=U_c-Ts,\\qquad G_c:=U_c-Ts+pV,\\qquad \\Omega_c:=U_c-Ts-\\sum_{j=1}^r\\mu_jn_j.$$\n\nTheir natural-coordinate charts are respectively $(s,p,n)$, $(T,V,n)$, $(T,p,n)$, and $(T,V,\\mu_1,\\ldots,\\mu_r)$. In the volume transform the conjugate coordinate is $-p=U_{c,V}$, which accounts for the plus sign in $H_c$ and $G_c$; in the chemical transform the conjugates are $\\mu_j=U_{c,n_j}$, which accounts for the minus signs in $\\Omega_c$. All four potentials have units of joules. A displayed differential or global convex-duality characterization is not part of this definition; critical, coexistence, and other non-C2 boundaries are excluded.",
          "supplier_status": "draft",
          "supplier_proof_status": "not-applicable",
          "evidence_scope": "Exact current interface excerpt; records citation fit and status, not independent proof approval."
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "supplier": "pthm-td-potential-differentials",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "excerpt": "On a valid local energy-representation chart from [[pthm-td-local-energy-representation]], let $s,V,n_j,U,T,p,\\mu_j$ and the potentials $H,F,G,\\Omega$ be as defined in [[def-td-legendre-potentials]]. On every open neighborhood where the corresponding natural-coordinate maps have their stated $C^1$ inverses, the potentials are C2 and their differentials are\n\n$$dU=T\\,ds-p\\,dV+\\sum_{j=1}^r\\mu_j\\,dn_j,\\qquad dH=T\\,ds+V\\,dp+\\sum_{j=1}^r\\mu_j\\,dn_j,$$\n\n$$dF=-s\\,dT-p\\,dV+\\sum_{j=1}^r\\mu_j\\,dn_j,\\qquad dG=-s\\,dT+V\\,dp+\\sum_{j=1}^r\\mu_j\\,dn_j,$$\n\n$$d\\Omega=-s\\,dT-p\\,dV-\\sum_{j=1}^r n_j\\,d\\mu_j.$$\n\nAll differentials are local state-function differentials. They do not assert that heat or work is an exact differential on process space.",
          "supplier_status": "draft",
          "supplier_proof_status": "ai-altered",
          "evidence_scope": "Exact current interface excerpt; records citation fit and status, not independent proof approval."
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "supplier": "pthm-td-local-energy-representation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "excerpt": "Let $c$ be a material constitution, let $r$ be its finite number of named species, and let $(U_0,V_0,n_{0,1},\\ldots,n_{0,r})$ lie in an open single-phase chart $\\Omega$ of physical SI coordinate values, with $U$ in joules, $V>0$ in cubic metres, and each $n_j>0$ in moles ([[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]], [[def-td-si-quantities]]). The chart is the image of an open subset of the dimensionless domain $D_c$ under the fixed diagonal unit conversion $(\\hat U,\\hat V,\\hat n)\\mapsto(U,V,n)$. Write $S_c(U,V,n)$ for the entropy on $D_c$ composed with the inverse unit conversion; its physical-coordinate partials include those unit factors as specified in [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]]. Assume this representative is $C^2$ and $S_{c,U}(U_0,V_0,n_0)>0$. Then there are open neighborhoods of $(S_c(U_0,V_0,n_0),V_0,n_0)$ and $(U_0,V_0,n_0)$ on which the equation\n\n$$S_c(U,V,n)=s$$\n\nhas a unique solution $U=\\mathcal U_c(s,V,n)$ of class $C^2$. On this local chart,\n\n$$\\mathcal U_{c,s}=T_c,\\qquad \\mathcal U_{c,V}=-p_c,\\qquad \\mathcal U_{c,n_j}=\\mu_{c,j},$$\n\nwith the intensive functions defined by [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]]. This is a local representation only: it asserts neither global injectivity or domain coverage nor differentiability at a nonsmooth phase boundary.",
          "supplier_status": "draft",
          "supplier_proof_status": "ai-generated",
          "evidence_scope": "Exact current interface excerpt; records citation fit and status, not independent proof approval."
        },
        {
          "fact": "L2",
          "supplier": "pthm-td-local-energy-representation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "excerpt": "Let $c$ be a material constitution, let $r$ be its finite number of named species, and let $(U_0,V_0,n_{0,1},\\ldots,n_{0,r})$ lie in an open single-phase chart $\\Omega$ of physical SI coordinate values, with $U$ in joules, $V>0$ in cubic metres, and each $n_j>0$ in moles ([[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]], [[def-td-si-quantities]]). The chart is the image of an open subset of the dimensionless domain $D_c$ under the fixed diagonal unit conversion $(\\hat U,\\hat V,\\hat n)\\mapsto(U,V,n)$. Write $S_c(U,V,n)$ for the entropy on $D_c$ composed with the inverse unit conversion; its physical-coordinate partials include those unit factors as specified in [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]]. Assume this representative is $C^2$ and $S_{c,U}(U_0,V_0,n_0)>0$. Then there are open neighborhoods of $(S_c(U_0,V_0,n_0),V_0,n_0)$ and $(U_0,V_0,n_0)$ on which the equation\n\n$$S_c(U,V,n)=s$$\n\nhas a unique solution $U=\\mathcal U_c(s,V,n)$ of class $C^2$. On this local chart,\n\n$$\\mathcal U_{c,s}=T_c,\\qquad \\mathcal U_{c,V}=-p_c,\\qquad \\mathcal U_{c,n_j}=\\mu_{c,j},$$\n\nwith the intensive functions defined by [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]]. This is a local representation only: it asserts neither global injectivity or domain coverage nor differentiability at a nonsmooth phase boundary.",
          "supplier_status": "draft",
          "supplier_proof_status": "ai-generated",
          "evidence_scope": "Exact current interface excerpt; records citation fit and status, not independent proof approval."
        },
        {
          "fact": "L2",
          "supplier": "thm-tdmath-local-implicit-rank",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "excerpt": "Let $1\\le q\\le n$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, and let $F:U\\to\\mathbb R^q$ be $C^1$ in the sense of [[def-c-one-map-and-local-inverse]]. Suppose $F(x_*)=0$ and some $q\\times q$ minor of the derivative matrix $DF(x_*)$ is invertible. This nonsingular-minor hypothesis is the explicit full-row-rank condition used here. If $q<n$, then after permuting coordinates $x=(u,v)\\in\\mathbb R^{n-q}\\times\\mathbb R^q$, there are open neighborhoods $P$ of $u_*$ and $Q$ of $v_*$ and a unique $C^1$ map $\\varphi:P\\to Q$ such that\n\n$$F(u,v)=0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad v=\\varphi(u)\\qquad((u,v)\\in P\\times Q).$$\n\nIts derivative is\n\n$$D\\varphi(u)=-D_vF(u,\\varphi(u))^{-1}D_uF(u,\\varphi(u)).$$\n\nThus the local zero set is a graph over $n-q$ free coordinates. If $q=n$, the zero is locally isolated, so the local graph dimension is zero. Here \u201clocal graph dimension $k$\u201d means a local graph parameterized by an open subset of $\\mathbb R^k$, with a singleton counted as dimension zero.",
          "supplier_status": "draft",
          "supplier_proof_status": "ai-altered",
          "evidence_scope": "Exact current interface excerpt; records citation fit and status, not independent proof approval."
        },
        {
          "fact": "L3",
          "supplier": "def-real-power",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "excerpt": "For $a>0$ and $x\\in\\mathbb R$, define\n$$a^x:=\\exp(x\\log a).$$\nFor the supplementary zero-base convention, define $0^x:=0$ when $x>0$.\n\nThe expressions $0^0$ and $0^x$ for $x<0$ are left undefined. Negative bases are not assigned arbitrary real powers.",
          "supplier_status": "published",
          "supplier_proof_status": "not-applicable",
          "evidence_scope": "Exact current interface excerpt; records citation fit and status, not independent proof approval."
        },
        {
          "fact": "L3",
          "supplier": "thm-real-power-continuity-and-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "excerpt": "For $a>0$, the function $x\\mapsto a^x$ is continuous on $\\mathbb R$ and\n$$(a^x)'=a^x\\log a.$$\nFor $\\alpha\\in\\mathbb R$, the function $x\\mapsto x^\\alpha$ is continuous and differentiable on $(0,\\infty)$, with\n$$(x^\\alpha)'=\\alpha x^{\\alpha-1}.$$",
          "supplier_status": "published",
          "supplier_proof_status": "ai-generated",
          "evidence_scope": "Exact current interface excerpt; records citation fit and status, not independent proof approval."
        },
        {
          "fact": "L4",
          "supplier": "thm-real-power-laws",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "excerpt": "For $a,b>0$ and $r,s\\in\\mathbb R$,\n$$a^{r+s}=a^ra^s,\\qquad (ab)^r=a^rb^r,\\qquad (a/b)^r=a^r/b^r,\\qquad (a^r)^s=a^{rs}.$$",
          "supplier_status": "published",
          "supplier_proof_status": "ai-generated",
          "evidence_scope": "Exact current interface excerpt; records citation fit and status, not independent proof approval."
        },
        {
          "fact": "L5",
          "supplier": "thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "excerpt": "Let $a<b$. Suppose $G:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b)$. If $f:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb R$ is Riemann integrable and\n\n$$f(x)=G'(x)\\qquad(a<x<b),$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\\int_a^b f=G(b)-G(a).$$\n\nNo derivative of $G$ at either endpoint is assumed, and the two endpoint values assigned to the integrable extension $f$ do not enter the conclusion.",
          "supplier_status": "published",
          "supplier_proof_status": "ai-altered",
          "evidence_scope": "Exact current interface excerpt; records citation fit and status, not independent proof approval."
        },
        {
          "fact": "L6",
          "supplier": "thm-chain-sum-product-and-composition-rules-for-banach-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "excerpt": "Let $U \\subseteq X$ be open in a real Banach space $X$, and let $Y$, $Z$, $W$ be\nreal Banach spaces. Then:\n\n1. **Sum rule.** If $f_1, f_2 : U \\to Y$ are Fr\u00e9chet differentiable at $x \\in U$\n   and $a, b \\in \\mathbb R$, then $af_1+bf_2$ is Fr\u00e9chet differentiable at $x$\n   with $D(af_1+bf_2)(x) = a\\,Df_1(x) + b\\,Df_2(x)$.\n2. **Bounded-bilinear product rule.** If $f : U \\to Y$ and $g : U \\to Z$ are\n   Fr\u00e9chet differentiable at $x$, and $B : Y \\times Z \\to W$ is bounded\n   bilinear, then $U \\ni u \\mapsto B(f(u),g(u))$ is Fr\u00e9chet differentiable at\n   $x$ and\n\n$$D\\bigl(B(f,g)\\bigr)(x)h = B\\bigl(Df(x)h,\\ g(x)\\bigr) + B\\bigl(f(x),\\ Dg(x)h\\bigr) \\qquad (h \\in X).$$\n\n3. **Chain rule.** If $f : U \\to Y$ is Fr\u00e9chet differentiable at $x$, if\n   $W_0 \\subseteq Y$ is an open set with $f[U] \\subseteq W_0$, and if\n   $g : W_0 \\to Z$ is Fr\u00e9chet differentiable at $f(x)$, then\n   $g \\circ f$ is Fr\u00e9chet differentiable at $x$ and\n   $$D(g \\circ f)(x) = Dg\\bigl(f(x)\\bigr)\\,Df(x).$$\n\nNo continuity of any derivative map is assumed; these are pointwise statements\nabout one $x$ at a time.",
          "supplier_status": "published",
          "supplier_proof_status": "ai-altered",
          "evidence_scope": "Exact current interface excerpt; records citation fit and status, not independent proof approval."
        },
        {
          "fact": "L7",
          "supplier": "def-natural-numbers",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "excerpt": "The set of **natural numbers** is the **smallest inductive set**\n([[def-inductive-set]]),\n\n$$\\mathbb{N} = \\omega := \\bigcap\\,\\{\\, I : I \\text{ is inductive} \\,\\},$$\n\nwhich exists and is itself inductive by [[lem-omega-smallest-inductive]] (the\nAxiom of Infinity, [[def-axiom-of-infinity]], supplies one inductive set to\nintersect within, and Separation, [[def-axiom-schema-of-separation]], makes the\nintersection a set). On $\\mathbb{N}$ we take\n\n$$0 := \\varnothing, \\qquad \\sigma(n) := n \\cup \\{n\\},$$\n\nthe distinguished element and the successor function. Thus\n$0 = \\varnothing$, $1 = \\{0\\}$, $2 = \\{0, 1\\}$, $3 = \\{0, 1, 2\\}$, and in general\n$n = \\{0, 1, \\dots, n-1\\}$ is the set of its predecessors.",
          "supplier_status": "published",
          "supplier_proof_status": "not-applicable",
          "evidence_scope": "Exact current interface excerpt; records citation fit and status, not independent proof approval."
        },
        {
          "fact": "L7",
          "supplier": "thm-induction-principle",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "excerpt": "Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{N}$. If $0 \\in S$ and $\\sigma(n) \\in S$ whenever $n \\in S$, then $S = \\mathbb{N}$. Equivalently (property form): if a property $P$ satisfies $P(0)$ and ($P(n) \\Rightarrow P(\\sigma(n))$) for all $n$, then $P(n)$ holds for all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$. This is the induction principle, the basis of proof by induction.",
          "supplier_status": "published",
          "supplier_proof_status": "ai-altered",
          "evidence_scope": "Exact current interface excerpt; records citation fit and status, not independent proof approval."
        },
        {
          "fact": "L7",
          "supplier": "def-ordered-field",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "excerpt": "An **ordered field** is a field $F$ ([[def-field]]) together with a subset\n$P \\subseteq F$, the set of **positive** elements (the *positive cone*),\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(O1) Trichotomy.** For each $x \\in F$, exactly one of the following holds:\n  $x \\in P$, or $x = 0$, or $-x \\in P$.\n- **(O2) Closure.** If $x, y \\in P$ then $x + y \\in P$ and $x \\cdot y \\in P$.\n\nWe write $x > 0$ for $x \\in P$ (read \"$x$ is positive\"), and define the order by\n\n$$a < b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; b - a \\in P, \\qquad a \\le b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; a < b \\text{ or } a = b.$$\n\nThus $a > b$ means $b < a$, and $a \\ge b$ means $b \\le a$. An element $x$ with\n$-x \\in P$ (equivalently $x < 0$) is called **negative**.",
          "supplier_status": "published",
          "supplier_proof_status": "not-applicable",
          "evidence_scope": "Exact current interface excerpt; records citation fit and status, not independent proof approval."
        }
      ]
    },
    "ex-td-finite-canonical-ground-state-entropy": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-td-si-quantities",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "This framework uses the modern International System of Units (SI). A scalar quantity has a real numerical value with the stated unit; a vector quantity has a value in the stated real coordinate space with the stated component unit. The numerical coordinates used elsewhere on this page are divided by their displayed SI units before they are placed in a Euclidean coordinate space.\n\nThe model's energy coordinate $U$ and its process-transfer values $Q$ and $W_{\\mathrm{on}}$ are scalar energies measured in joules (J). Volume $V$ is a scalar measured in cubic metres (m\u00b3); amount of substance $n_j$ for species $j$ is a scalar measured in moles (mol); and a particle count $N_j$, when separately used in a microscopic model, is dimensionless. Time $t$ is measured in seconds (s), mass $M$ in kilograms (kg), and a translational velocity $\\mathbf v_{\\mathrm{cm}}$ is a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$ with components measured in metres per second (m/s). The physical meanings of $U,Q,W_{\\mathrm{on}}$ are fixed by the state and process definitions, not by their units alone.\n\nThe relevant exact SI relations are\n\n$$1\\,\\mathrm J=1\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m^2\\,s^{-2}},\\qquad 1\\,\\mathrm N=1\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-2}},\\qquad 1\\,\\mathrm{Pa}=1\\,\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}}.$$\n\nThe kelvin (K) is the SI unit of thermodynamic temperature. The Boltzmann constant has the exact defining value $k_{\\mathrm B}=1.380649\\times10^{-23}\\,\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$. The mole is the SI unit of amount of substance; the Avogadro constant has the exact defining value $N_{\\mathrm A}=6.02214076\\times10^{23}\\,\\mathrm{mol^{-1}}$. When a model uses particle counts, $n_j=N_j/N_{\\mathrm A}$. The molar gas constant is derived as $R=N_{\\mathrm A}k_{\\mathrm B}=8.31446261815324\\,\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}\\,K^{-1}}$. This framework does not set $k_{\\mathrm B}=1$. These SI definitions do not themselves introduce a thermodynamic temperature state function or entropy function.\n\nWithin the macroscopic framework, $(U,V,n_j)$ are adopted state coordinates. A quantity's status as primitive in this model is separate from whether its SI unit is a base or derived unit.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a material constitution $c$ and its equilibrium state domain $D_c$ from [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. Let $\\Omega\\subseteq D_c$ be a specified open, single-phase chart in physical SI coordinates $(U,V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r)$, with $U$ evaluated in the center-of-mass rest frame of [[def-td-galilean-rest-frame]]. On $\\Omega$, use the finite $C^2$ entropy function $S_c$ and the strict positivity $S_{c,U}>0$ adopted by [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]]. Here $C^2$ means that all coordinate partial derivatives through order two exist and are continuous ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]). A coordinate partial such as $S_{c,U}$ is the ordinary derivative in that coordinate direction while every other physical coordinate is held fixed, as in [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]].\n\nDefine the **absolute thermodynamic temperature**, **entropic pressure**, and **molar chemical potential** of species $j$ by\n\n$$T_c:=\\frac{1}{S_{c,U}},\\qquad p_c:=T_cS_{c,V},\\qquad \\mu_{c,j}:=-T_cS_{c,n_j}.$$\n\nThe entropy unit is $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$, the energy $U$ is in joules, $V$ is in cubic metres, and $n_j$ is in moles ([[def-td-si-quantities]]). Thus $S_{c,U}$ has units $\\mathrm{K^{-1}}$, $T_c$ is finite and positive in kelvins, $S_{c,V}$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}\\,m^{-3}}$ and $p_c$ has units pascals, and $S_{c,n_j}$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}\\,mol^{-1}}$ and $\\mu_{c,j}$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}}$. If a microscopic model instead uses a dimensionless particle count $N_j=N_{\\!A}n_j$, the corresponding per-particle chemical potential is $\\mu_{c,j}/N_{\\!A}$ joules, with the exact SI Avogadro constant from [[def-td-si-quantities]].\n\nThese are scalar state functions of the stated rest-frame equilibrium chart. The pressure in this definition is a derivative-defined conjugate; identifying it with force per area requires a separate adopted mechanical coupling. Neither an empirical thermometer calibration nor any equation of state is supplied by this definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-natural-logarithm",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, define $\\log x$ to be the unique real $y$ such that $\\exp(y)=x$. This is well-defined because [[cor-exponential-is-a-bijection-onto-positive-reals]] states that $\\exp:\\mathbb R\\to(0,\\infty)$ is a bijection.\n\nThus $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is the inverse function of $\\exp$. In particular, $\\log(\\exp y)=y$ for every $y\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\exp(\\log x)=x$ for every $x>0$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-natural-logarithm-laws",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The function $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous and strictly increasing, is onto $\\mathbb R$, and satisfies, for $x,y>0$,\n$$\\log(xy)=\\log x+\\log y,\\qquad \\log(x/y)=\\log x-\\log y,\\qquad \\log(1/x)=-\\log x.$$\nAlso $\\log 1=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-exponential-limits-and-range",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "$$\\exp(x)\\to+\\infty\\quad(x\\to+\\infty),\\qquad \\exp(x)\\to0\\quad(x\\to-\\infty),$$\nand the range of $\\exp$ is contained in $(0,\\infty)$ and is unbounded above with infimum $0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-logarithm-slower-than-every-positive-power",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every $\\alpha>0$,\n$$\\lim_{x\\to+\\infty}\\frac{\\log x}{x^\\alpha}=0.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derive-1-1",
          "claim": "For each excited state $i\\notin G:=\\{i:E_i=E_0\\}$, $(E_i-E_0)/(k_{\\mathrm B}T)\\to+\\infty$, so $w_i(T)\\to0$ by [F5]. Ground-state weights are $1$. Since there are finitely many states, $Z(T)\\to g$, each ground-state probability tends to $1/g$, and each excited-state probability tends to $0$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F5",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-2-1",
          "claim": "By continuity of $\\log$ at $1/g>0$ from [F4], the total ground-state contribution to entropy tends to\n$\n-k_{\\mathrm B}g\\frac1g\\log(1/g)=k_{\\mathrm B}\\log g.\n$",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-2-2",
          "claim": "For an excited state with $p_i(T)\\to0$, set $x=1/p_i(T)\\to+\\infty$. By [F4], $p_i\\log p_i=-\\log(x)/x$, which tends to $0$ by [F6]. The finite sum of excited-state contributions therefore tends to $0$.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F6",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-3-1",
          "claim": "Combining the ground and excited contributions in the finite sum gives $S_G(T)\\to k_{\\mathrm B}\\log g$. In the $g=1$ case this limit is $0$; for $g>1$ it is positive because $\\log$ is strictly increasing and $\\log1=0$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 2.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
