    "ex-td-geometric-cooling-protocol": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-td-si-quantities",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "This framework uses the modern International System of Units (SI). A scalar quantity has a real numerical value with the stated unit; a vector quantity has a value in the stated real coordinate space with the stated component unit. The numerical coordinates used elsewhere on this page are divided by their displayed SI units before they are placed in a Euclidean coordinate space.\n\nThe model's energy coordinate $U$ and its process-transfer values $Q$ and $W_{\\mathrm{on}}$ are scalar energies measured in joules (J). Volume $V$ is a scalar measured in cubic metres (m\u00b3); amount of substance $n_j$ for species $j$ is a scalar measured in moles (mol); and a particle count $N_j$, when separately used in a microscopic model, is dimensionless. Time $t$ is measured in seconds (s), mass $M$ in kilograms (kg), and a translational velocity $\\mathbf v_{\\mathrm{cm}}$ is a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$ with components measured in metres per second (m/s). The physical meanings of $U,Q,W_{\\mathrm{on}}$ are fixed by the state and process definitions, not by their units alone.\n\nThe relevant exact SI relations are\n\n$$1\\,\\mathrm J=1\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m^2\\,s^{-2}},\\qquad 1\\,\\mathrm N=1\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-2}},\\qquad 1\\,\\mathrm{Pa}=1\\,\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}}.$$\n\nThe kelvin (K) is the SI unit of thermodynamic temperature. The Boltzmann constant has the exact defining value $k_{\\mathrm B}=1.380649\\times10^{-23}\\,\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$. The mole is the SI unit of amount of substance; the Avogadro constant has the exact defining value $N_{\\mathrm A}=6.02214076\\times10^{23}\\,\\mathrm{mol^{-1}}$. When a model uses particle counts, $n_j=N_j/N_{\\mathrm A}$. The molar gas constant is derived as $R=N_{\\mathrm A}k_{\\mathrm B}=8.31446261815324\\,\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}\\,K^{-1}}$. This framework does not set $k_{\\mathrm B}=1$. These SI definitions do not themselves introduce a thermodynamic temperature state function or entropy function.\n\nWithin the macroscopic framework, $(U,V,n_j)$ are adopted state coordinates. A quantity's status as primitive in this model is separate from whether its SI unit is a base or derived unit.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a material constitution $c$ and its equilibrium state domain $D_c$ from [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. Let $\\Omega\\subseteq D_c$ be a specified open, single-phase chart in physical SI coordinates $(U,V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r)$, with $U$ evaluated in the center-of-mass rest frame of [[def-td-galilean-rest-frame]]. On $\\Omega$, use the finite $C^2$ entropy function $S_c$ and the strict positivity $S_{c,U}>0$ adopted by [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]]. Here $C^2$ means that all coordinate partial derivatives through order two exist and are continuous ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]). A coordinate partial such as $S_{c,U}$ is the ordinary derivative in that coordinate direction while every other physical coordinate is held fixed, as in [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]].\n\nDefine the **absolute thermodynamic temperature**, **entropic pressure**, and **molar chemical potential** of species $j$ by\n\n$$T_c:=\\frac{1}{S_{c,U}},\\qquad p_c:=T_cS_{c,V},\\qquad \\mu_{c,j}:=-T_cS_{c,n_j}.$$\n\nThe entropy unit is $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$, the energy $U$ is in joules, $V$ is in cubic metres, and $n_j$ is in moles ([[def-td-si-quantities]]). Thus $S_{c,U}$ has units $\\mathrm{K^{-1}}$, $T_c$ is finite and positive in kelvins, $S_{c,V}$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}\\,m^{-3}}$ and $p_c$ has units pascals, and $S_{c,n_j}$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}\\,mol^{-1}}$ and $\\mu_{c,j}$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}}$. If a microscopic model instead uses a dimensionless particle count $N_j=N_{\\!A}n_j$, the corresponding per-particle chemical potential is $\\mu_{c,j}/N_{\\!A}$ joules, with the exact SI Avogadro constant from [[def-td-si-quantities]].\n\nThese are scalar state functions of the stated rest-frame equilibrium chart. The pressure in this definition is a derivative-defined conjugate; identifying it with force per area requires a separate adopted mechanical coupling. Neither an empirical thermometer calibration nor any equation of state is supplied by this definition.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-natural-numbers",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The set of **natural numbers** is the **smallest inductive set**\n([[def-inductive-set]]),\n\n$$\\mathbb{N} = \\omega := \\bigcap\\,\\{\\, I : I \\text{ is inductive} \\,\\},$$\n\nwhich exists and is itself inductive by [[lem-omega-smallest-inductive]] (the\nAxiom of Infinity, [[def-axiom-of-infinity]], supplies one inductive set to\nintersect within, and Separation, [[def-axiom-schema-of-separation]], makes the\nintersection a set). On $\\mathbb{N}$ we take\n\n$$0 := \\varnothing, \\qquad \\sigma(n) := n \\cup \\{n\\},$$\n\nthe distinguished element and the successor function. Thus\n$0 = \\varnothing$, $1 = \\{0\\}$, $2 = \\{0, 1\\}$, $3 = \\{0, 1, 2\\}$, and in general\n$n = \\{0, 1, \\dots, n-1\\}$ is the set of its predecessors.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-induction-principle",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{N}$. If $0 \\in S$ and $\\sigma(n) \\in S$ whenever $n \\in S$, then $S = \\mathbb{N}$. Equivalently (property form): if a property $P$ satisfies $P(0)$ and ($P(n) \\Rightarrow P(\\sigma(n))$) for all $n$, then $P(n)$ holds for all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$. This is the induction principle, the basis of proof by induction.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-ordered-field",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "An **ordered field** is a field $F$ ([[def-field]]) together with a subset\n$P \\subseteq F$, the set of **positive** elements (the *positive cone*),\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(O1) Trichotomy.** For each $x \\in F$, exactly one of the following holds:\n  $x \\in P$, or $x = 0$, or $-x \\in P$.\n- **(O2) Closure.** If $x, y \\in P$ then $x + y \\in P$ and $x \\cdot y \\in P$.\n\nWe write $x > 0$ for $x \\in P$ (read \"$x$ is positive\"), and define the order by\n\n$$a < b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; b - a \\in P, \\qquad a \\le b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; a < b \\text{ or } a = b.$$\n\nThus $a > b$ means $b < a$, and $a \\ge b$ means $b \\le a$. An element $x$ with\n$-x \\in P$ (equivalently $x < 0$) is called **negative**.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
